The problem is that this kind of code is correct :

void *memcpy(void *s1, const void *s2, size_t n) ;

So "const void *" is different of "void *"

a function returning  "void *" does say that you can do :

char *ptr = memcpy(zone, "hello", 6) ;
ptr[3] = 'T' ;

but you cannot do :

const void *search_in_a_const_zone_first_null(const void *zone) ;
char *ptr = search_in_a_const_zone_first_null(zone) ;
ptr[1] = 'T' ;

egcs -Wall -Wcast-qual for example says that my exemple is correct.
HPUX, SOLARIS, Unixware, AIX compilers are OK with this.

Now I realise that this warning says 'your const attribute is ignored'.
So if I want the compiler checks that const invariance is preserved, I
bet that Digital CXX doesn't do the job. It's quite a pity.
The solution to hide these warnings is not an option for me.

typedef void * Opaque ;
typedef const void *const_Opaque ;
Opaque MyMemcpy(Opaque, const_Opaque, size_t) ;

The solution of having to declare 2 typedefs for each type (with/without
the const qualifier) is quite ugly dont you think ?



Eric Dumazet

