I sent a fuller response to Eric via email, but for anyone following
along at home:

Eric Dumazet <edumazet@cosmosbay.com> writes:
> egcs -Wall -Wcast-qual for example says that my exemple is correct.
> HPUX, SOLARIS, Unixware, AIX compilers are OK with this.

egcs, Sun Workshop, and any other compiler that aspires to standards
compliance will interpret

typedef void *Opaque;
const Opaque func(void);

as

void* const func(void);

because that is what standard C semantics require.

> Now I realise that this warning says 'your const attribute is ignored'.
> So if I want the compiler checks that const invariance is preserved, I
> bet that Digital CXX doesn't do the job. It's quite a pity.

The difference is that cxx is (IMHO) doing a much better job--it at
least tells you that const in that position doesn't do a darn thing.
The other compilers interpret it the same way, they just don't tell
you that it doesn't do anything.
