If I understand correctly you want to boot the computer, but you have
broken it by setting the default runlevel to 5.
If this is the case, the from the >>> prompt you should type
boot -flags s
to cause the machine boot into single user mode.
From there you should be able to mount your filesystems with
bcheckrc ; swapon -a
then you should be able to edit your inittab to correct the problem.

Hope this helps
Adam
PS: If you don't select a default runlevel in /etc/inittab, init will
prompt for a runlevel at boot time. This can be useful when debugging
start up configurations.
