david minor wrote:

> - vdump from current rz0. Physically pull it out of the machine.
> disklabel the new drive as rz0 and partition it in the sizes we want.
> vrestore the directories into their new home.
> 
> - vdump from current rz0. disklabel rz0 something else (doesn't matter
> what). disklabel the new drive as rz0 and partition it in the sizes we
> want. vrestore the directories into their new home.

First of all, there is no such thing as "disklabel the new drive as
rz0". You simply disklabel the drive and its number depends on it's SCSI
ID and a SCSI controller number.

rz0  - SCSI0 ID0
rz1  - SCSI0 ID1
....
rz8  - SCSI1 ID0
....

So, you can have both drives in the machine, disklabel the second *the
way you want it*, create any file systems you like (don't forget the
swap area) and do "vdump | vrestore" thing. No tapes needed.

> ps. this is also in anticipation of upgrading from 3.2c to 4.0e

There are a lot of changes and there is an upgrade path to be followed.

3.2c - 3.2g - 4.0a - 4.0b/c - 4.0d - 4.0e

Nix




