Hi, this one is because of NT, the time in the real time clock has become
corrupt (NT and unix use the information in different ways).
You can use the date command to fix this, then do a clean reboot and all
should be OK.
If not, then at the >>> prompt you can also set the date (you may have
problems post y2k).
If the machine still comes up to single user mode then you can get to multi-
user mode by typing init 3 from single user mode, and can set the machine to
boot to the default runlevel in /etc/inittab by setting the os boot flags
to a (can't remember the variable name sorry).

Hope this helps
Adam







