Massimo Gais <mgais@na.astro.it> writes:

> Now just a question about terms: why 255.255.254.0 is called
> "supernet-ting"?

It depends on the network class you are using. 255.255.254.0 (/23) is
a supernet of a class C network, but a subnet of a class A or B
network.

If you want to do this with something < 4.0D, you can do as described
below.

/Johan

------------------------------------------------------------
From: joda@pdc.kth.se (Johan Danielsson)
Subject: Re: SUBNET MASK on FDDI Addendum
Date: 10 Nov 1997 01:16:17 +0100


The kernel won't allow you to make subnets larger than the former
classes. This is a brain-damage that should go away - there are no
classes!

First, why do you want all your machines on the same subnet? There are
really no advantages with this. If you have machines that doesn't know
anything about subnets and routes, you might consider using proxy-arp.

If you want to do this anyway, you can add net-routes for the
`subnets' that the 8200 isn't on.

For instance:

------------------------------------------------------------
# ifconfig fta0 192.168.1.1 netmask 0xffffff00
# route add 192.168.2.0 192.168.1.1 -interface
add net 192.168.2.0: gateway 192.168.1.1
# route add 192.168.3.0 192.168.1.1 -interface
add net 192.168.3.0: gateway 192.168.1.1
# netstat -rn
Routing tables
Destination      Gateway            Flags     Refs     Use  Interface
Netmasks:
Inet             255.255.255.0      
Inet             255.255.255.224    

