Have you tried mounting the AdvFS fileset with the -d option?  If you
try this, mount it read-only.  If it works, then backup your data and
rebuild your domain.  Do not continue to use a fileset mounted with -d.

If you can not get your fileset mounted, I would suggest contacting
your Customer Support Center and asking for assistance from them.  That
is their job after all.

By the way, any system panic should be reported (software should not
panic except for hardware failures).  If there is an existing patch,
then you can and install it.  If this is a new problem, then
engineering can be notified and a patch can be created.

At the very minimum you could request from the CSC a copy of the AdvFS
"salvage" utility (getting the most recent version is always a good
idea since it is constantly being improved).  The salvage utility can
be used to recover what it can from a corrupt AdvFS domain.

Good luck



