Looks like a bad SCSI hard disk.  Although I'm not really familiar with
CAM terminology (so I'm not *sure* how you'd convert this info into the
appropriate device name), I assume that the "TARGET x0" means that the
affect disk has SCSI ID 0... probably your boot disk.  You can scan your
disks for bad blocks using the "scu" program.  For example, as root...

scu -f /dev/rrz0a

...will let you run tests on, and manipulate, the hard disk associated
with /dev/rrz0a.  Once you're inside scu just enter "help" at the "scu>"
prompt and you can look at the available options.  Verifying goes
something like "verify media starting 0 length 100000" if you want to
verify 100,000 blocks starting at block 0.

You could try reseating the cables, but most likely you would either
need to back up the disk and do a low-level format with scu, or throw
the disk away and get a new one.

