In article <7n7pq5$2tf$1@nnrp1.deja.com>, ivan.lopes@rio.rsl.com.br writes:

>We are getting some error messages in uerf and syslog.dated but
>I can't tell for sure what they mean.
>

It would be useful to know what version you're running, since I'd
expect modern versions to include the logical number of the device
with the error.

>CLASS                         x0000     DISK
>SUBSYSTEM                     x0000     DISK
>BUS #                         x0001
>                                        TARGET x0

The logical unit on bus 1, SCSI ID 0 has the error.

>----- CAM STRING -----
>
>ROUTINE NAME                            cdisk_complete

The error was noticed and reported by the routine cdisk_complete.
Presumably a completion routine for the CAM disk driver.

>----- CAM STRING -----
>
>                                        Retries Exhausted

It retried the I/O request some unknown number of times, but
finally gave up.

>----- CAM STRING -----
>
>ERROR TYPE                              Hard Error Detected

A bit of a catch-all for many error types.

>
>----- CAM STRING -----
>
>DEVICE NAME                             DEC     HSZ40           V31Z

The logical unit is being presented to the host by an HSZ40 running
V3.1 of the HSZ firmware.  A bit dated, but not too old.

>
>----- CAM STRING -----
>
>                                        Active CCB at time of error
>----- CAM STRING -----
>
>                                        Target selection timeout

The "Active CCB" is attempting to report the CAM driver's internal
data structure that was used for the I/O request.  The target
selection timeout, a SCSI protocol problem and is a bit surprising
in this context.

>
>ERROR - os_std, os_type = 11, std_type = 10

I believe this is Uerf's error because it couldn't translate some
part of the error packet.

>----- ENT_CCB_SCSIIO -----
>
>*MY ADDR                  x0EDCF100
>CCB LENGTH                    x00C0
>FUNC CODE            x01
>CAM_STATUS                    x000A     CAM_SEL_TIMEOUT
>PATH ID              1.
>TARGET ID            0.
>TARGET LUN           0.
>CAM FLAGS                 x00000482
>                                        CAM_QUEUE_ENABLE
>                                        CAM_DIR_OUT
>                                        CAM_SIM_QFRZDIS
>
>*PDRV_PTR                 x0EDCEDA8
>*NEXT_CCB                 x00000000
>*REQ_MAP                  x0632F2C0
>VOID (*CAM_CBFCNP)()      x0056AAC0
>*DATA_PTR                 x45BE6800
>DXFER_LEN                 x00000800
>*SENSE_PTR                x0EDCEDD0
>SENSE_LEN            xA0
>CDB_LEN              x06
>SGLIST_CNT                    x0000
>CAM_SCSI_STATUS               x0000     SCSI_STAT_GOOD
>SENSE_RESID          x00
>RESID                     x00000000
>CAM_CDB_IO           x00000000000000042404000A
>CAM_TIMEOUT               x0000003C
>MSGB_LEN                      x0000
>VU_FLAGS                      x4000
>TAG_ACTION           x20

This is the SCSI CAM CCB structure associated with the I/O request.  Much
of the information is only of interest to those maintaining the CAM driver,
but some bits of information that we gleam from it are that it was a six
byte SCSI command (CDB_LEN), that this particular SCSI command completed
with a "Good" status (CAM_SCSI_STATUS) and the transfer size seems to have
been 2048 bytes (0x800 in DXFER_LEN).

Now, I'll go out on a bit of limb and guess that it was a six byte write
command (the 0A at the end of the CAM_CDB_IO).  Considering that LSM
says it is write error, this is a pretty safe guess.

So, if the "Target selection timeout" is any clue, the operating system
sent a write request with 2048 bytes of data, but the device didn't
respond appropriately within the timeout period.

