ijkdc@hotmail.com wrote:
> 
> Hello,
>   I'm trying to compile a C program with Digital C.  Unfortunately I
> didn't write the program which will make it harder to debug and the
> author doesn't seem to have any experience with non-GNU compilers.  The
> program compiles fine on my Linux box with gcc but has trouble with
> Digital C on OSF/1.  I get the following error:
> 
> cc: Error:  line 299: In this declaration, "sin" has no
> linkage and is of an incomplete type.
>         struct sockaddr_in sin;  /* used to store inet addr stuff  */
> ---------------------------^
> 
> I don't really understand the error message and I've never declared a
> struct like that.  I would have to agree that it's an incomplete type,
> but gcc doesn't seem to mind it.  Could someone shed some light on this
> situation please?

The error message indicates that the definition of 'struct sockaddr_in' is
missing.  You probably need to '#include <netinet/in.h>'.

When trying to solve such problems, there's a foolproof way of getting to
the bottom of things. Use the compiler flags

	-source_listing -show all

along with regular parameters.  The (pre-)compiler will write a <file>.lis
output file which you can open with an editor.  Simply look for the
first occurance of the symbol in question.  I suspect in this case the
first occurance will be in the source module 'icq_response.c', which means
'struct sockaddr_in' never gets defined.

cheers,

Rob



