I suppose gettimeofday() dont use this hack because there is a problem
when the timeval structure is modified (probably by clock interrupt,
1024 per second)
If your application reads tv.tv_sec and tv.tv_usec, it couls be
interrupted by the clock and you could have something like :

printf("first : %d.%06d\n", ptr->tv.tv_sec, ptr->tv.tv_usec) ;
printf("next : %d.%06d\n", ptr->tv.tv_sec, ptr->tv.tv_usec) ;

first : 931810701.999024
next : 931810701.000000  (interrupt between read of tv_sec and tv_usec)


In order to have correct results, the gettimeofday-like function should
:
read tv_sec in a local, then read tv_usec in an other local,
then reread tv_sec to see if a wrap occured. If not, OK, else return to
begin

So be carefull !
Eric Dumazet


