>>I recently took over a couple of Digital systems, both 3.2G and 4.0D,
>>which for some reason sends messages from crontab jobs to root, like
>>this:
>>   [.. snip...]
>>
>>The line in cron is as follows:
>>
>>00,10,20,30,40,50 * * * * /usr/local/bin/supervision >>
>>/var/tmp/supervision/supervision.log 2>&1
>>
>>Can something in the script being run override the redirection? Or how
>>can I get mail with this redirection? Can something in the setup of cron
>>change this?

  Shells process redirection in the order encountered (or at least /bin/sh
  does so).

  Thus... you redirect standard output to the file, and _then_ redirect
  the STDERR stream back to the STDOUT stream -- which is still the
  original STDOUT -- namely your "terminal", _not_ the redirected stream.

  Try doing the   "2>&1"  before the ">>" and see what happens.
  It works for me.

