Hallo!

 > TCP mem-mem:            11.24Mbyte/second
 > NFSv3 sequential read:   3.39Mbyte/second
 > NFSv3 sequential write:  4.14Mbyte/second

 > Check your cabling, network settings, and network
 > configuration. Full-duplex vs. half-duplex mismatch is one common source
 > of problems with NFS performance; having more than two NICs on a single
 > full-duplex segment in another. Start with:

Our cabling is perfectly well, all cards are running full duplex and are
connected directly to a Cisco switch with STP cables. There is no TCP/IP
throughput problem at all, ftp, X11, several benchmarks and all other
services yield marvellous throughput rates, even if I enforce situations
where full duplex is fully used.

If I just copy a large file over NFS, I get the same throughput rates that you
do. Only that benchmarks like this usally have little to do with the real
world situation.

The problem I was speaking of occurs when working with many small files, like
it is done in real world situations. Like unpacking a source code tarball
onto a NFS drive, like compiling source code, etc. pp. This is what our users
are doing most of the time, and it simply is too slow given what kind of
hardware we have as server. 

I take my performance measures by using a large source code tarball (like the
emacs sources) and unpacking it from NFS to a local disk (fast) and from a
local disk to a NFS disk (terribly slow). This benchmark shows why NFS feels
so slow for our users even though we have acceptable plain throughput rates.

I suspect the problem has to do with the NFS server waiting for the file
being written entirely to the local hard disk before sending an
acknowledgement and the client waiting for that acknowledgement before
transferring the next file, or something like that (I am not firm in NFS
protocol internals). 

And again, swapping the DEC server with a Linux server (of much less powerful
hardware) gives a factor three speed improvement. 

Kind Regards,
Tobias.



