Tobias Ernst (tobi@bland.fido.de) wrote:
: If I just copy a large file over NFS, I get the same throughput rates that you
: do. Only that benchmarks like this usally have little to do with the real
: world situation.

It happens to be the "real world" preferred usage pattern for *our* users :-)

: I suspect the problem has to do with the NFS server waiting for the file
: being written entirely to the local hard disk before sending an
: acknowledgement and the client waiting for that acknowledgement before
: transferring the next file, or something like that (I am not firm in NFS
: protocol internals). 

NFS requires that each write request is commited to media before a success
indication can be returned by the server. If most of your traffic is create/
write/close for a bunch of *small* files, I guess a single-thread performance
is going to be limited by the round-trip time.

If you think you are limited by this, try mounting your NFS file system 
(and the underlying UFS file system if you are using UFS) with nosync
option. It is a *very* good idea to make your NFS mounts hard,nintr if
you use this. 

It this does not help, try running sys_check on both the client and the
server during the high-load time...

Regards,

/Serge.P

