patchkov@acs1.acs.ucalgary.ca (Serguei Patchkovskii) writes:

> NFS requires that each write request is commited to media before a success
> indication can be returned by the server. If most of your traffic is create/
> write/close for a bunch of *small* files, I guess a single-thread performance
> is going to be limited by the round-trip time.

	Well, yes and no. NFS2 works as described, NFS3 has the ability for the
	client to commit the data, ie. delay the disk write confirmation. I do
	not remember offhand if the commit was to be done per file or per client.

	We have had situations when our NFS3 mounts have dropped to NFS2 mounts,
	and only indication is poor NFS write speed.

	Run nfsstat both on client and server and see how nfs2 and nfs3 protocols
	are used.
