Tobias Ernst <tobi@bland.fido.de> writes:


>The problem is a performance problem. Reading from the NFS server is quite
>fast, but writing, especially with many smaller files, very slow.

>For testing purposes we replaced the 500au server with a P200 box with 64 MB
>RAM running Linux, and, without optimizing anything, instantly got a speed
>improvement of factor 3 in the writing direction. This really astonished us.

Linux is more interested in speed than sanctity of your data and does
deferred I/O in places where Tru64 does synchronous I/O.  If Tru64
says it created the file, it will still be there if power goes away a
millisecond later.  It's a lot easier to figure out who to sue if our
NFS server loses your files than if a Linux server loses them.

I'm not certain, but I think even with AdvFS, you have to wait for
synchronous writes of the log file.  You do win on fewer seeks, but
AdvFS does not make creating files a CPU-only exercise.

>Could you give us some hints on what to do to improve the situation or on how
>to debug it properly?

One of the best things to do is install a Prestoserve board.  It can
be configured to hold synchronously written data in its NVRAM instead
of waiting for it to be written to disk.  Caveat - can't be used on
a ASE cluster because the cached data can't be failed over to the
other server.  Also, some high end disk controllers (e.g. HSZs) have
a NVRAM cache that performs similarly.
