Reply-To: alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com (Alan Rollow - Dr. File System's Home for Wayward Inodes.)
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.unix.osf.osf1


It depends on the file system, the content and what licenses you have.

To resize UFS, you have to backup the data, resize the partition and
restore the data.  Not necessarily in that order if you have a spare
disk.

To resize AdvFS, there is an elegant shortcut if you have the Utilities
license.  For any file system but the root, you can add a partition to
the domain for the file system and then remove the original.  AdvFS
will migrate the data to the new space.  For the root you still have
to create the new domain by hand, then backup/restore.

If you can't keep the spare disk online during the migration, it is
little different than restoring a backup after a system crash.  The
system management documentation may have guidelines for how to do
that.

On some versions, it is wise to run verify on the domain before using
addvol/rmvol to migrate data.
