Hi, I upgraded from 4.0C to 4.0D about 6 months ago, and at the same
time went from a small disk to a larger one, this is what I did. There
are probably some things that could be done better. This was on a
Personal Workstation 433au.


First good news for you is that there is no need to remove / relabel the
disks in the machine. 

Your steps are 
1 configure the 4 GB disk
  1.1 Make sure there is nothing you want on it.
  1.2 (Assuming you are using Common Desktop Environment 'CDE')
      Anyone who has experience with 3.2c please check this
      Select the application manager 
         select system admin
           select configuration
             select disk utility
               now select your 4 GB disk and partition it
               NB stay with BSD ufs

2 copy old disk to new
  Use vdump to copy everything on your old disk to the corresponding
areas on the new disk.
  Configure the swap partition on the new disk

3 fix the /etc/fstab file on the new disk

  #/dev/rz16b     swap1   ufs    sw  0  2
  #/dev/rz16a     /       ufs    rw  1  1
  #/dev/rz16g     /usr    ufs    rw  1  2
  /dev/rz16a      /root_old       ufs    rw 1 1
  /dev/rz16g      /usr_old       ufs    rw 1 1

  /dev/rz18a      /       ufs    rw 1 1
  /dev/rz18g      /usr    ufs    rw  1  2
  /dev/rz18b      swap2   ufs    sw 0 2


3 test the new disk
  Shutdown your computer.
  at the >>> prompt you can BOOT whichever disk you want
  check how to do this with HELP BOOT  (my machine is up so I cant)

  Test the the new disk works OK 

  Shutdown and change the BOOT disk using a SET command
    SET DEFAULT_BOOTDEV or something like that
  again check the >>> HELP

NB it is sometimes usefull to have two system disks, even if one is an
older version of the OS. Just make sure you comment out the bits of
fstab for all other disks

  eg on your old system disk comment out anything in /etc/fstab that
doesnt refer to the old system disk.


Hope this helps.

-- 

Keith Moodie,

moodiek@dnr.qld.gov.au



