> ... [uerf] output format that is simultaneously so hard for a human to
> read and so hard to grovel through with tools like grep.
> Does someone out there have a tool for browsing binary.errlog ...

Yes: try perl. I often use something like:

  /usr/sbin/uerf -c err -o full | uerf.sanitize

where uerf.sanitize is the following perl script:

#!/usr/local/bin/perl -w
# Sanitize 'uerf -o full' output:
#  remove disk spin-up events (when 'Disk spin down' is set with dxpower)
sub mypr {
  return if $X eq '';
  return if $X =~ m/ CAM SCSI \n.* cdisk_rec_status \n.*\n   *Recovery progress event, this is NOT \n   *_an error \n/s;
  print $X;
}
$X = '';
while (<>) {
  &mypr, $X='' if /^\*\*\*/;
  $X .= $_;
}
&mypr;

