> Peter Mayne (Peter.Mayne@compaq.com) wrote:
> : uerf is obsolete. Depending on your platform, you should be using DECevent
> : (the dia command) or, if you have a DS10/DS10, Compaq Analyze.
>
> Yes, uerf is obsolete, and one should be using DECevent.  However, it
doesn't
> answer the original poster's question, since DECevent uses the same output
> format as uerf does.  I haven't seen Compaq Analyze, though.
>
>         Stu

No it doesn't. uerf stacks data up on the right hand side, with lots of line
wrapping, while dia starts things on the left hand side so they stay on one
line, so it's easier to grep for data (since UNIX lacks $SEARCH/WINDOW
functionality).

dia also shows more information in a more readable format than uerf. I've got
two windows side-by-side with uerf in one and dia in the other, and I'd rather
read the dia output. (Personal preference, of course.)

Further, I believe that uerf is not supported on some more recent platforms
where dia is supported.

The original post said

> I mean, it takes true skill to come up with an output format that is
> simultaneously so hard for a human to read and so hard to grovel
> through with tools like grep.

Well, dia is easier for a human to read and easier to use grep on.

PJDM
