>No it doesn't. uerf stacks data up on the right hand side, with lots of line
>wrapping, while dia starts things on the left hand side so they stay on one
>line, so it's easier to grep for data (since UNIX lacks $SEARCH/WINDOW
>functionality).

|% man cut
|User Commands                                              cut(1)
|
|NAME
|     cut - cut out selected fields of each line of a file
|
[...]

Speaking of cutting, could you post some sample dia output?

Here's the problem I have with uerf output:

|********************************* ENTRY     1. ********************************
|
|----- EVENT INFORMATION -----
|
|EVENT CLASS                             ERROR EVENT 
|OS EVENT TYPE                  199.     CAM SCSI 
|SEQUENCE NUMBER              64214.
|OPERATING SYSTEM                        DEC OSF/1 
|OCCURRED/LOGGED ON                      Tue Jan 12 18:40:07 1999
|OCCURRED ON SYSTEM                      grendel 
|SYSTEM ID                 x0007001B
|SYSTYPE                   x00000000
|
|----- UNIT INFORMATION -----
|
|CLASS                         x0022     DEC SIM 
|SUBSYSTEM                     x0000     DISK 
|BUS #                         x0003
|                              x00C8     LUN x0
|                                        TARGET x1
|
|********************************* ENTRY     2. ********************************

Regardless of what side things are lined up, grepping this will give you
data that's just too hard to correlate to events. I am working on a Tcl
script that parses this and turns it into a stream that looks like:

event.section-n.tag: text

|% uerf | ua 'CLASS'
|1.EVENT INFORMATION-0.EVENT CLASS: ERROR EVENT
|1.UNIT INFORMATION-0.CLASS: x0022     DEC SIM
|2.EVENT INFORMATION-0.EVENT CLASS: ERROR EVENT
|2.UNIT INFORMATION-0.CLASS: x0000     DISK

THIS is greppable. I don't understand why this stuff needs to be stuck in
binary files in the first place, but a tool like my 'ua' is something that
really ought to be a standard part of the toolchain.

When I've got it tidied up I'll post it.

